>> MF=2 RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                      9440 1451     
                                                                  9440 1451     
  *** ORNL Evaluation, July 2010                                  9440 1451     
  H.Derrien, L.C.Leal, D.Wiarda                                   9440 1451     
                                                                  9440 1451     
A reevaluation of the resonance parameters was performed at ORNL  9440 1451     
by Bouland and Derrien(NSE 127, 105-109, 1997) in the energy range9440 1451     
thermal to 5.7 keV(1994-1995). The results of this evaluation     9440 1451     
were adopted for JEFF, but does not appear in ENDF/B-VI and VII.  9440 1451     
In order to calculate the covariance files in the resolved region 9440 1451     
a new evaluation was performed in 2009-2010 by Derrien et al.     9440 1451     
Compared to the Bouland-Derrien evaluation, the high resolution   9440 1451     
transmission data taken at ORELA in 1977 by Harvey and Gwin were  9440 1451     
added to the SAMMY data base.  These data were not analysed by the9440 1451     
authors and were ignored in the Bouland-Derrien evaluation due to 9440 1451     
lack of information. The samples used by Harvey-Gwin were thicker 9440 1451     
than those used in the GELINA transmission measurements (Kolar et 9440 1451     
a1, J. Nuclear Energy, 22,299,968) which were the basis of the    9440 1451     
identification of the resonances in the Bouland-Derrien           9440 1451     
evaluation. Consequently much more resonances were identified     9440 1451     
in the new evaluation mainly p-wave resonances. Due to a lack of  9440 1451     
time, Weston fission data were not included in the data base: the 9440 1451     
fission widths obtained in the Bouland-Derrien evaluation were    9440 1451     
kept for most of them. All the experimental data available at     9440 1451     
thermal energies were included in the new evaluation.             9440 1451     
  The cross sections in the energy range from thermal to 5700 ev  9440 1451     
neutron energy were well represented by 554 resonances: 549       9440 1451     
resonances in the energy range 0 to 5700 eV, 5 fictitious negative9440 1451     
resonances to take into account the contriobution of the bound    9440 1451     
levels, and 4 fictitious resonances above 5700 eV for the         9440 1451     
contributions of the higher energy resonances. The value of       9440 1451     
9.65 fm was obtained for the scattering radius R'; this include   9440 1451     
the effects of the far away resonances. More than 90% of the 428  9440 1451     
s-wave resonances were identified from their shape. The 121 other 9440 1451     
resonances were assigned p-wave rersonances, although few of them 9440 1451     
could be d-wave resonances.                                       9440 1451     
 The distribution of the s-wave reduced neutron widths corresponds9440 1451     
to a Porter-Thomas distribution with an average value of 0.0012   9440 1451     
ev, taking into account the possibility of missing 12 small       9440 1451     
levels. The corresponding average level spacing and neutron       9440 1451     
srength function are Do=12.9 eV and So=0.0000926.  The 121        9440 1451     
resonances assigned p-wave resonances correspond to a small part  9440 1451     
of the p-wave resonance population(about 10%).  However, if one   9440 1451     
assume that all the p-wave resonances with a reduced neutron width9440 1451     
larger than 0.01 eV (the largest p_wave resonances) have been     9440 1451     
identified, an estimated value of S1=0.000149 can be extrapolated 9440 1451     
from the distribution of these large reduced neutron widths. In   9440 1451     
the Atlas of Neutrn Resonances(Mughabghab) one find Do=12.8 eV(in 9440 1451     
agreement with the cpresent evaluation),So=0.000111(12% larger),  9440 1451     
and S1=0.00028(about two times larger).                           9440 1451     
 The cross sections at 0.0253 eV neutron energy, calculated at    9440 1451     
300 K by SAMMY are the following:                                 9440 1451     
                                                                  9440 1451     
            Capture        282.25 +- 2.0 b                        9440 1451     
            Scattering       1.77 +- 0.02 b                       9440 1451     
            Fission          0.037+- 0.02 b                       9440 1451     
                                                                  9440 1451     
            Total          284.05 +- 2.0 b                        9440 1451     
                                                                  9440 1451     
 In the thermal range the experimental total of Spencer et al.    9440 1451     
(Spencer et al.. Nucl. Sci. Eng., 96, 318, 1987) had the largest  9440 1451     
weight in the SAMMY fits.                                         9440 1451     
 The Resonance Parameter Covariance Matrix(RPCM) was calculated   9440 1451     
from the SAMMY sequential analysis of the experimental data base  9440 1451     
after energy scale, normalization, and background corrections     9440 1451     
leading to a consistent experimental data base.  The RPCM allows  9440 1451     
the calculation of the uncertainties on cross sections due to the 9440 1451     
statistical uncertainties of the experimental data.               9440 1451     
 An estimation of the systematic uncertainties was made from      9440 1451     
preliminary SAMMY runs allowing the variation of experimental     9440 1451     
parameters particularly the experimental normalization and        9440 1451     
background. Actually in the sequential SAMMY analysis of all      9440 1451     
the experimental data allowing the variation of the experimental  9440 1451     
parameters a Complete Parameters Covariance Matrix (CPCM) is      9440 1451     
calculated.  SAMMY can use the CPCM to calculate the group cross  9440 1451     
section covariances matrices and the corresponding total          9440 1451     
uncertainties.  The CPCM (combining resonance parameters and      9440 1451     
experimental pasrameters) cannot be used in the ENDF/B format.    9440 1451     
 In the present evalution the systematic uncertainties on the     9440 1451     
average cross sections were estimated for 52 groups in the energy 9440 1451     
range thermal to 5700 eV adapted to the fluctuations of average   9440 1451     
cross sections at least in the low energy range.  For all the     9440 1451     
cross sections, the correlation coefficients were taken egal to   9440 1451     
0.8, to take into account that some systematic uncertainties could9440 1451     
not be fully correlated. The Cross Section Covariane Matrix       9440 1451     
(CSCM) were generated in ENDF/B format for the 52 group total,    9440 1451     
elastic scattering, capture and fission cross sections.           9440 1451     
                                                                  9440 1451     
